---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import FeatureGrid from "../components/FeatureGrid.astro";
import FaqSection from "../components/FaqSection.astro";
import EditorApp from "../components/EditorApp.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Fit Full Photo on Instagram Without Cropping | InstaDL"
  description="Fit your full photo on Instagram without cropping. Easily resize landscape and vertical photos to Instagram aspect ratios using borders and blur backgrounds."
>
  <EditorApp />
  
  <Hero
    title="Fit Full Photos on Instagram Without Cropping"
    description="Prevent Instagram from auto-cropping your portrait or landscape images. Add sleek borders or smart blurred margins to preserve your complete photo in the feed."
  />

  <FeatureGrid />

  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 lg:py-32 border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6">
      <div class="flex flex-col items-center text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink mb-6">
          Resolving Instagram's Vertical Cropping Limits
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
          Instagram enforces strict aspect ratio limits for posts: 1:1 for squares, 4:5 for vertical portraits, and 1.91:1 to 16:9 for horizontal landscapes. If you upload a standard smartphone photo (which is typically 3:4 or 9:16), Instagram forces you to crop the top and bottom, losing vital parts of your composition.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-start border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Why Portrait Photos Get Cut Off</h3>
          <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Vertical photos captured on most modern smartphones are shot in a 4:3 or 16:9 aspect ratio. However, Instagram's feed restricts vertical posts to a maximum height of 4:5. This means any photo taller than 4:5 will be auto-cropped, cutting off heads, feet, or important background details.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-body leading-relaxed">
            By padding your tall portrait shots with borders or smart blurred backgrounds using our resizer, you can transform your vertical images into the ideal 4:5 format. This guarantees that your entire photo shows up on your followers' feeds exactly as you shot it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">How to Post Full Widescreen Photos</h3>
          <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Widescreen landscape images suffer from the opposite problem. While Instagram allows horizontal posts, they often look very small in the feed, getting buried under larger vertical posts. 
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-body leading-relaxed">
            Fitting horizontal photos into a 1:1 square or 4:5 portrait frame with styled side paddings makes them stand out significantly more. Our tool processes all resizing operations 100% locally in your browser, keeping your images private and preserving high resolution.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection />
</Layout>
